#import "/_lib/0.1.1.typ": *
#show: template

// #taxon[Taxon]
#title[Formalization Plan]

Show
$
  "Rectangle"(S) ~= sum_(R_1, R_2 : "Eq-Rel"(S)) "is-equiv" lambda s . (r_1 (s), r_2 (s)) ~= "Car-Dec"(S)
$

Let $T(S) :-= sum_(R_1, R_2 : "Eq-Rel"(S)) "is-equiv" underbrace(lambda s . (r_1 (s), r_2 (s)), -= : f)$.

For $"Rectange"(S) ~= T(S)$:
Use that
$
  product_(x,y : S) "is-contr"(sum_(z: S) R_1 (x,z) xx R_2 (y,z)) ~= "is-equiv" ( f )
$

with
```agda
-- forward direction
is-equiv-is-contr-map : is-contr-map f -> is-equiv f -- is-contr-map is in module block

-- back
is-contr-map-is-equiv : is-equiv f → is-contr-map f
```

Use `is-effictive-quotient-map` to show the fiber of f at $([x]_R_1, [y]_R_1)$
is
$
  sum_(z: S) R_1 (x,z) xx R_2 (y,z).
$

For $T(S) ~= "Cart-Dec"(S)$: 
For forward direction:
Given $(R_1, R_2, h)$, with $h : "is-equiv"(f)$, set
$
  A :-= S slash R_1,\
  B :-= S slash R_2,\
  e :-= f : S ~= A xx B\
$

and $p,q$ `is-surjective-quotient-map`.

For backward direction:
Given $(A,B,e,p,q)$, set
$
  R_1 (x,y) :-= (pi_1 (e(x)) = pi_1 (e (y)))
  R_2 (x,y) :-= (pi_2 (e(x)) = pi_2 (e (y)))
$

use "isomorphism theorem"
```agda
is-set-quotient-is-surjective-and-effictive
```
Apply this to $pi_1 @ e$ and $pi_2 @ e$ to get
$
  A ~= S slash R_1\
  B ~= S slash R_2
$

composing with $e$ gives and equivalence $S ~= S slash R_1 xx S slash R_2$ for the $"is-equiv"$ condition for $T(S)$

Now check round trip
$
  T(S) -> "Cart-Dec"(S) -> T(S)
$

After roundtrip we get
$
  (S slash R_1, S slash R_2, e') .. "with" e'(s) = ([s]_R_1, [s]_R_2)
$

The isomorphismtheorem gives
$
  S slash R_1 ~= S slash "ker"(pi_1 @ e) ~=
$

to identity $"ker"(pi_1 @ e)$ with $R_1$ use `propositional-extensionality`
$
  "ker"( pi_1 @ e) <==> pi_1 (e (x)) = pi_1 (e(y)) <==> [x]_R_1 = [y_R_1] <==> R_1 (x,y)
$

then use`equiv-uniqueness-set-quotient` / `is-set-quotient-uniqueness` to idenity the quotients.
Tuple equality with `univalence`

The other round trip
$
  "Cart-Dec" -> T -> "Cart-Dec"
$
We get $(S slash R_1, S slash R_2, e')$ where $R_1 = "ker"(pi_1 @ e)$, $R_2 = "ker" (pi_2 @ e)$. with the isomorphism theorem we get
$
  alpha: A ~= S slash R_1,\
  beta : B ~= S slash R_2,\
  e' @ (alpha xx beta) = e
$
Then we get the identification with `univalance` and propositional-extensionality for the surjectivity proofs.



Usefull things:
```
-- from foundation-core.contractible-maps
is-contr-map-is-equiv : is-equiv f → is-contr-map f
is-equiv-is-contr-map : is-contr-map f -> is-equiv f -- is-contr-map is in module block


-- from foundation.effective-maps-equivalence-relations
is-effective :
  {l1 l2 l3 : Level} {A : UU l1} (R : equivalence-relation l2 A) {B : UU l3}
  (f : A → B) → UU (l1 ⊔ l2 ⊔ l3)
is-effective {A = A} R f =
  (x y : A) → (f x ＝ f y) ≃ sim-equivalence-relation R x y

-- from foundation.set-quotients
module _
  {l1 l2 : Level} {A : UU l1} (R : equivalence-relation l2 A)
  where
  abstract
    is-effective-quotient-map : is-effective R (quotient-map R)

-- from  foundation.set-quotients
quotient-map : A → set-quotient
quotient-map = set-quotient-equivalence-class ∘ class R

is-surjective-quotient-map : is-surjective quotient-map
is-surjective-quotient-map =
  is-surjective-left-comp-equiv compute-set-quotient (is-surjective-class R)


-- proposional extionality stuff is in
-- foundation.propositional-extensionality

-- univalence things are in
-- foundation.univalence

-- foundation.uniqueness-set-quotients
equiv-uniqueness-set-quotient : type-Set B ≃ type-Set C
equiv-uniqueness-set-quotient =
  pr1 (center uniqueness-set-quotient)


-- foundation.universal-property-set-quotients
is-set-quotient-is-surjective-and-effective :
  ( E :
    is-surjective-and-effective R
      ( map-reflecting-map-equivalence-relation R q)) →
  is-set-quotient R B q
```
